<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Cleartext is permitted so the relay can be reached over plain HTTP/WS in the
  one case the browser still allows it from the app's secure (https) page:
  a LOOPBACK relay (http://127.0.0.1:8000, e.g. `adb reverse` in dev, or a local
  Orbot SOCKS/HTTP proxy), which Chromium treats as potentially trustworthy.

  CORRECTED 2026-07-29 (pentest M-4). This file used to claim a `.onion` was
  "also potentially trustworthy". THAT IS FALSE FOR CHROMIUM, which is what the
  WebView is. Measured:

    | context                                      | isSecureContext | crypto.subtle |
    | Chromium, http://<addr>.onion                | false           | ABSENT        |
    | Firefox, default prefs                       | false           | ABSENT        |
    | Firefox + dom.securecontext.allowlist_onions | true            | present       |
    | Chromium, http://127.0.0.1                   | true            | present       |

  Only Firefox has that pref, and only Tor Browser ships it enabled. From the
  app's https page `new WebSocket("ws://<addr>.onion")` throws outright ("An
  insecure WebSocket connection may not be initiated from a page loaded over
  HTTPS"), and cleartextTrafficPermitted cannot help: it is an Android-platform
  control that sits BELOW Chromium's mixed-content blocker. So pointing the app
  at the onion is NOT "an on-device task, not a code change" as PROGRESS.md
  claimed — it cannot work, with or without Orbot.

  DO NOT "fix" this by loading http://<addr>.onion as the WebView's DOCUMENT
  origin. That is a Critical regression twice over: it removes crypto.subtle
  entirely (no secure context => no Web Crypto => the client cannot run at all),
  and it makes the JavaScript server-delivered again, destroying the exact H-02
  property the Android app exists to provide — code you verified once and
  bundled in the APK, rather than re-fetched from a relay you do not trust.

  The workable shapes, both still to be built:
    * a local Orbot HTTP/SOCKS proxy on 127.0.0.1 (a trustworthy origin)
      fronting the onion — which is why loopback stays allow-listed below; or
    * wrapping the onion in TLS so the page can use wss:// directly.

  The `onion` entry is kept on purpose: it is inert (the mixed-content blocker
  refuses those connections before this file is consulted) and it is what a
  loopback Orbot proxy would eventually forward TO. Removing it would change
  nothing about what the app can reach today.

  A plain http:// LAN host (192.168.x.x) is likewise blocked by the
  mixed-content rule regardless of this setting, so such a relay simply will not
  connect (the app shows a clear error). For a clearnet production relay, front
  it with TLS: the page then uses wss:// and no cleartext is involved.

  This does NOT affect chat security either way: all confidentiality/authenticity
  is end-to-end inside the bundled client; the relay only sees opaque ciphertext.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <!-- Default: no cleartext anywhere. A clearnet relay must be https/wss. -->
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false" />
    <!-- Loopback is the only host cleartext actually reaches today; see above
         for why the .onion entry is retained but cannot currently be used. -->
    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <domain includeSubdomains="true">127.0.0.1</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="true">localhost</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="true">onion</domain>
    </domain-config>
</network-security-config>
